ration(idx)\n        elif nextchar == \"[\":\n            return _TokenizingJSONArray((string, idx + 1), context.strict, _scan_once, content)\n        elif nextchar == \"]\":\n            raise StopIteration(idx)\n        ...\n```\n\nI think something like that. So `{\"a\": }` \u2192 scan_once at '}' \u2192 StopIteration \u2192 JSONDecodeError(\"Expecting value\"). And `{\"a\":` \u2192 scan_once at idx beyond string \u2192 IndexError \u2192 StopIteration \u2192 same.\n\nOK here's my plan: use get_info to confirm module path and available helpers, then write tests that call `_TokenizingJSONObject` directly with a small custom scan_once (self-contained, no dependence on scanner internals), plus maybe one or two tests through the public `tokenize_json` for integration. Actually, the user wants tests that execute lines 29-95; direct calls suffice. But adding public-API tests could be nice for robustness. However, \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 just imports and test functions.\n\nRegarding cleanup: direct calls with locally-created memo dicts \u2192 no global state. The module-level WHITESPACE regex is stateless. No monkeypatching needed. But the user emphasizes cleanup; I'll note that each call uses fresh memo/content, no monkeypatching needed. Maybe I don't need monkeypatch at all. But to be safe, nothing global is touched.\n\nWait \u2014 one consideration: `scanstring` from json.decoder with strict=True. For keys with escape sequences, e.g. `{\"a\\nb\": 1}` \u2014 scanstring handles it. Not needed for coverage.\n\nLet me now also double-check the ScalarToken constructor: `ScalarToken(value, start, end, content)`. From typesystem.tokenize.tokens:\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start, end, content):\n        self.value = value\n        self.start = start\n        self.end = end\n        self.content = content\n    ...\n```\n\nAnd ScalarToken(Token). There's also `Token.get_value()`, `Token.__eq__` maybe. Let me check with get_info(\"ScalarToken\") and get_info(\"Token\").\n\nNow the custom scan_once: I want it to mimic the real one closely enough. For values in my tests: integers and strings. Let me write:\n\n```python\ndef _make_scan_once(content: str):\n    \"\"\"Return a minimal scan_once callable supporting strings and integers.\"\"\"\n\n    def scan_once(s: str, idx: int) -> typing.Tuple[ScalarToken, int]:\n        try:\n            char = s[idx]\n        except IndexError:\n            raise StopIteration(idx) from None\n\n        if char == '\"':\n            value, end = scanstring(s, idx + 1, True)\n            return ScalarToken(value, idx, end - 1, content), end\n        if char.isdigit() or (char == \"-\" and s[idx + 1 : idx + 2].isdigit()):\n            start = idx\n            end = idx + 1\n            while end < len(s) and (s[end].isdigit() or s[end] in \".eE+-\"):\n                end += 1\n            text = s[start:end]\n            value = float(text) if any(c in text for c in \".eE\") else int(text)\n            return ScalarToken(value, start, end - 1, content), end\n        raise StopIteration(idx)\n\n    return scan_once\n```\n\nHmm, careful with `s[idx+1:idx+2].isdigit()` when idx+1 beyond \u2192 '' \u2192 isdigit() False. OK.\n\nWait, there's an issue with the number loop including '+'/'-' \u2014 for '1}' it stops at '}'. For '1,' stops. Fine. But `s[end] in \".eE+-\"` could over-consume in weird cases; my test inputs are simple.\n\nActually, simpler: only support non-negative integers and strings. My test values: 1, 2. And maybe a string value to test string values: `{\"a\": \"x\"}`. Let me include that in one test for good measure (not required for coverage of 29-95 but harmless). Actually keep it minimal but correct.\n\nHmm, wait. Should I instead use the module's real scanner? Let me check what's importable. If the module has `_make_scanner(context, content)`, I could do:\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import _make_s