      "reasoning_content": "Key findings:\n\n1. `_TokenizingJSONObject` is confirmed \u2014 signature matches the snippet. Module path: the user says `typesystem/tokenize/tokenize_json.py`. get_info didn't show the module path for `_TokenizingJSONObject` explicitly, but `tokenize_json` function and `_make_scanner` exist. The `tokenize_json` function references `_TokenizingDecoder(content=content)` \u2014 interesting, so there's a `_TokenizingDecoder` class. The module is likely `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json` per the user. Hmm, but get_info output for `_make_scanner` didn't show a path either. Only ScalarToken/Token/DictToken showed \"in typesystem/tokenize/tokens.py\".\n\nLet me be careful about the import path. The user explicitly said \"extracted from typesystem/tokenize/tokenize_json.py\". I'll import from `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json`. \n\n2. Important: `Token.__init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content='')` \u2014 attributes are `_value`, `_start_index`, `_end_index`, `_content`, with properties `value`, `string`, `start`, `end`. Note `start`/`end` are `Position` objects, not ints! So my assertions about positions should use `token._start_index` / `token._end_index` or compare `token.value`. Position has line_no, column_no, char_index presumably.\n\nSo for assertions:\n- `key.value == 'a'` (property value \u2192 _get_value()).\n- `key._start_index == 1`, `key._end_index == 3`.\n- Or `key.start.char_index == 1`? Position likely has char_index. Safer to use `_start_index`/`_end_index` \u2014 but those are private. Hmm. Alternatively use `token.string` property \u2014 `string` probably returns `self._content[self._start_index:self._end_index + 1]`. For key ScalarToken('a', 1, 3, content): string = content[1:4] = '\"a\"'. That's a nice public assertion!\n\nLet me verify: Token.string property \u2014 likely:\n\n```python\n@property\ndef string(self) -> str:\n    return self._content[self._start_index : self._end_index + 1]\n```\n\nThat's the typical implementation in typesystem. So for the key token in '{\"a\":1}': content[1:4] = '\"a\"'. For value ScalarToken(1, 5, 5): string = content[5:6] = '1'. \n\nI can assert `key.string == '\"a\"'` and `value.string == '1'` and `value.value == 1`. These are public API assertions. But I'm not 100% sure of the `string` implementation. Risky. Alternatively use `_start_index`/`_end_index` directly \u2014 private but stable in this codebase version. Hmm.\n\nActually, since `Token.__init__` stores `_start_index` etc., and the properties `start`/`end` return `Position` objects (via `_get_position`), I could assert `key.start.char_index == 1` if Position has char_index. From `tokenize_json`: `Position(column_no=exc.colno, line_no=exc.lineno, char_index=exc.pos)` \u2014 so Position has char_index, line_no, column_no. And `_get_position(index)` probably computes line/column from content and sets char_index=index. So `key.start.char_index == 1` should work. But again, guessing.\n\nSafest: assert on `.value` (public, certain) and use `_start_index`/`_end_index` for position checks (certain from the shown `__init__`). Private attribute access in tests is acceptable, and the get_info output confirms these attribute names exist. I'll use a mix: `.value` assertions primarily, plus `_start_index`/`_end_index` in a couple of tests.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 also `Token.__eq__` exists. What does it compare? Probably `self._value == other` or comparing tokens. Uncertain \u2014 avoid relying on `==` for tokens except maybe `token.value == ...`.\n\n3. `_make_scanner(context, content)` exists and returns `scan_once` which wraps `_scan_once` with `finally: memo.clear()`. Note: `memo.clear()` in the outer `scan_once` \u2014 the memo is `context.memo`. The context is a JSONDecoder-like object with `.memo`, `.strict`, `.parse_string`, `.parse_array`, `.parse_float`, `.parse_int`. There's `_TokenizingDecoder(content=content)` in the module \u2014 a subclass of json.JSONDecoder that sets memo and content presumably.\n\nSo for integration tests, I can use `tokenize_json(content)` which returns a Token (DictToken for objects). And `DictToken._get_value()` returns dict of plain values presumably: `{key: value._value ...}`. The `value` property returns `self._get_value()`.\n\nFor `tokenize_json('{\"a\": 1}')` \u2192 DictToken; `token.value` should be `{'a': 1}`. That's a clean integration assertion.\n\nAlso `tokenize_json` raises `ParseError` (from typesystem.base) on JSONDecodeError. For integration error tests: `tokenize_json('{1: 2}')` \u2192 ParseError with text \"Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes.\" and position char_index=1. ParseError has `.message` property probably. Hmm, typesystem ParseError: `ParseError(text=..., code=..., position=...)` and has `.message` property that formats with position. Let me not over-assert: assert `excinfo.value.code == 'parse_error'` maybe. Hmm, is `code` an attribute? Base `Position`... `ParseError(text, code, position)` \u2014 typesystem.base.Message has `text`, `code`, `position` attributes and `message` property. I think `exc.text` and `exc.code` exist. Reasonably confident.\n\nBut to minimize risk, for integration tests I'll assert on successful parses only (token.value == {...}), and maybe one error test asserting ParseError is raised and `exc.code == \"parse_error\"`. Actually, let me check: in typesystem/base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, *, text, code, position=None):\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.position = position\n        self._key = None\n        self._index = None\n\n    def __eq__(...)\n    @property\n    def message(self):\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd `ParseError(Message)`. So `.text`, `.code`, `.position` exist. Good.\n\nNow, decision on test structure:\n\nPrimary tests: direct calls to `_TokenizingJSONObject` with a custom minimal scan_once. This is fully self-contained and covers all lines/branches.\n\nIntegration tests: use `tokenize