       return ScalarToken(value, idx, end - 1, content), end\n        if char.isdigit():\n            start = idx\n            end = idx + 1\n            while end < len(s) and s[end].isdigit():\n                end += 1\n            return ScalarToken(int(s[start:end]), start, end - 1, content), end\n        raise StopIteration(idx)\n    return scan_once\n```\n\nScalarToken(value, idx, end-1, content) \u2014 matches _make_scanner's usage: `ScalarToken(value, idx, end - 1, content)`. Good.\n\nNow assertions. For direct tests, the returned dict maps key-ScalarToken \u2192 value-ScalarToken. To extract: `[(k.value, v.value) for k, v in result.items()]`. `.value` is a property \u2192 `_get_value()`. For ScalarToken, `_get_value` returns `self._value` presumably. Good.\n\nPosition assertions: `key._start_index`, `key._end_index`. Confirmed attribute names from Token.__init__. I'll use them sparingly (one test) to avoid over-reliance... Actually the user wants assertions verifying postconditions; value assertions + end index of the function return are the key postconditions. I'll include token index assertions in a couple of tests using `_start_index`/`_end_index` \u2014 they're confirmed to exist.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider using `.string` property: `Token.string` \u2014 likely `self._content[self._start_index:self._end_index + 1]`. If I assert `key.string == '\"a\"'` and the implementation differs (e.g., strips quotes), the test breaks. `_start_index` is safer. Use `_start_index`/`_end_index`.\n\nNow write the full test list (final):\n\n1. `test_empty_object` \u2014 `{}` \u2192 ({}, 2). Lines 37T/38F/42T/43.\n2. `test_compact_object` \u2014 `{\"a\":1}` \u2192 fast paths: 37F, 48-53, 56F, 60, 62/63F, 70-71, 74-76, 77F, 82-84T/85, 95. Assert pairs, end, key/value token indices.\n3. `test_whitespace_around_key_colon_and_value` \u2014 `{ \"a\" : 1 }` \u2192 38-40T, 56T/57/58F, 63T/65F, 77T/78-79. Assert pairs + end.\n4. `test_multiple_whitespace_after_colon` \u201