ood.\n\n13. `test_tokenize_json_error_integration` \u2014 `tokenize_json('{1: 2}')` \u2192 pytest.raises(ParseError), assert `exc.code == \"parse_error\"` and `exc.position.char_index == 1`. ParseError import from `typesystem.base`. Position attribute `char_index` \u2014 confirmed from tokenize_json source constructing `Position(column_no=..., line_no=..., char_index=exc.pos)`. So Position has char_index. And Message stores position. Good.\n\nHmm, but is `code` an attribute of Message? From the constructor call `ParseError(text=..., code=..., position=...)` \u2014 yes, it accepts code kwarg; Message stores `self.code = code` almost certainly. I'll assert on `exc.text` too? text = exc.msg + '.' = \"Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes.\" Safe to assert `exc.code == \"parse_error\"` and `exc.position.char_index == 1`. I'll assert both but keep it minimal. Actually, to reduce risk, assert `exc.position.char_index == 1` and `\"property name\" in exc.text`. Both attributes are near-certain.\n\nHmm, actually, should I include integration tests at all? They add coverage of lines 29-95 through the real path too, and verify wiring. The user asked for tests executing lines 29-95 \u2014 both approaches do. I'll include them; they're valuable and low-risk given get_info confirmed `tokenize_json` and ParseError usage.\n\nWait, one concern about `tokenize_json` with bytes: not needed.\n\nAnother concern: `tokenize_json('{\"a\": 1}')` \u2014 decoder = _TokenizingDecoder(content=content); decoder.decode(content) \u2192 json.JSONDecoder.decode \u2192 self.raw_decode \u2192 scan_once... The `scan_once` wrapper clears memo in finally. Fine, no state pollution.\n\nNow \u2014 cleanup/state pollution: \n- Direct tests: fresh memo dict per call, no globals touched. \n- Integration tests: tokenize_json creates its own decoder per call. \n- No monkeypatching needed. I'll note nothing global is mutated. The user said \"use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\" \u2014 i