 user says the module file is `tokenize_json.py`. I'll go with `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json`.\n\nHmm, one risk: if the actual installed typesystem has `typesystem/tokenize/json.py`, the import fails. But the user's statement is authoritative for their codebase version. Also `tokenize_json` function name matches module name \u2014 plausible. Go with it.\n\nNow, about `typing` import in test \u2014 used for type hints in helpers. Fine, or drop hints to keep it simple. I'll keep light hints.\n\nAlso: \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 only imports + helpers + tests. Helpers at module level are fine (they're not \"top-level code\" in the sense of executed script logic; but to be safe keep them minimal).\n\nLet me also double check `ScalarToken` constructor arg order: from _make_scanner: `ScalarToken(value, idx, end - 1, content)` \u2192 (value, start_index, end_index, content). And Token.__init__(value, start_index, end_index, content=''). Good.\n\nOne more consideration for the custom scan_once: strict param \u2014 I hardcode True in scanstring call; the direct tests pass strict=True anyway. Fine.\n\nAlso for the number branch in my mini scanner: only digits (no negative/float needed since my test values are 1 and 2). Keep it simple but correct: handle optional leading '-'? Not needed. Keep digits only \u2014 simpler and correct for the tests. But \"each test is correct\" \u2014 the helper must behave correctly for the inputs used. Digits-only is fine.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 should the mini scanner handle the case where `char == '\"'` uses `scanstring(s, idx + 1, True)`? Yes, mirroring _make_scanner: `parse_string(string, idx + 1, strict)` where parse_string = context.parse_string = scanstring. Good.\n\nEdge: in test 8, scan_once(s, 5) with len(s)=5 \u2192 s[5] IndexError \u2192 StopIteration(5). \u2713. In a hypothetical `{\"a\": }` direct test \u2014 not included; integration covers it? I won't include it; test 8 covers 72-73.\n\nNow the integration tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_json_integration():\n    token = tokenize_json('{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert token.value == {\"a\": 1}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_json_parse_error_integration():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as excinfo:\n        tokenize_json(\"{1: 2}\")\n    assert excinfo.value.code == \"parse_error\"\n    assert excinfo.value.position.char_index == 1\n```\n\nHmm, `token.value` for DictToken \u2014 `_get_value()` implementation unknown but DictToken stores `_child_tokens = {k._value: v for k, v in self._value.items()}` and `_get_value` likely returns `{key: token._value for key, token in self._child_tokens.items()}` or similar \u2192 {\"a\": 1}. Confident enough.\n\nActually hold on, let me reconsider `excinfo.value.position.char_index`. ParseError \u2192 Message with position=Position(column_no, line_no, char_index). `exc.pos` = 1 \u2192 char_index=1. \u2713. And `code=\"parse_error\"`. \u2713.\n\nAlso `excinfo.value.text` would be \"Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes.\" \u2014 I could assert `\"property name\" in excinfo.value.text`. Add it for robustness of the assertion. Eh, keep code + char_index.\n\nNow, one thing I want to double-check: `tokenize_json` requires non-empty content \u2014 fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 potential issue: `tokenize_json` is also the name of the module. `from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import tokenize_json` \u2014 imports the function, shadowing nothing problematic. Fine.\n\nLet me also think about whether to add a test for whitespace-before-key via integration (`{ \"a\": 1 }`) \u2014 not necessary.\n\nNow, \"make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution\" \u2014 I'll add a brief comment noting each call uses fresh memo/content and no global state is mutated, so no monkeypatching is required. Maybe I can also add a defensive fixture? Not needed. Actually \u2014 one subtle thing: `json.decoder.scanstring` \u2014 the C version is used; stateless. OK.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider one thing: in `_call` helper I create memo fresh each time \u2014 good. But should I also verify that the memo dict passed in is actually used/populated? Postcondition: after parsing `{\"a\": 1}`, memo contains 'a'. That verifies line 52's memo_get with setdefault actually stores. Let me assert that in test 2: pass memo explicitly and assert `memo == {\"a\": \"a\"}`. That's a nice postcondition for the memo logic. Let me restructure: `_call(s, start=1, strict=True, memo=None)` returning (result, end) and expose memo. Or in test 2, call _TokenizingJSONObject directly:\n\n```python\ndef test_compact_object():\n    s = '{\"a\":1}'\n    memo = {}\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject((s, 1), True, _make_scan_once(s), memo, s)\n    assert [(k.value, v.value) for k, v in result.items()] == [(\"a\", 1)]\n    assert end == len(s)\n    key = next(iter(result))\n    assert (key._start_index, key._end_index) == (1, 3)\n    value = result[key]\n    assert (value._start_index, value._end_index) == (5, 5)\n    assert memo == {\"a\": \"a\"}\n```\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me also double check `k.value` \u2014 ScalarToken._get_value returns self._value \u2192 'a' / 1. \u2713.\n\nAnd dict iteration order: insertion order \u2014 pairs appended in order. \u2713.\n\nOne more possible branch: line 63 `if s[end] in _ws` \u2014 when s[end] is the last char and in ws, then line 65 `s[end]` after end+=1 could IndexError \u2192 covered by 67-68 too. E.g., `{\"a\": ` (trailing space, len 6): end=5, s[5]=' ' \u2192 end=6; s[6] \u2192 IndexError \u2192 pass; scan_once(s,6) \u2192 StopIteration(6) \u2192 \"Expecting value\" at 6. My test 8 uses `{\"a\":` which covers 67-68 via the first s[end] access. The nested IndexError (line 65's s[end]) is also caught by the same except \u2014 same lines 67-68. Fine, covered.\n\nWhat about line 77's IndexError vs line 76? Both in same try \u2192 80-81. Covered by test 9.\n\nI think the test set is complete. Let me also consider: does line 30-31 (`pairs_append = pairs.append`) count as covered \u2014 yes, executed on every call.\n\nLine 35: `nextchar = s[end:end+1]` \u2014 every call. \u2713\n\nNow, one more thought on the \"improves coverage\" requirement per test:\n1. empty object: 37T,38F,42T,43 + return \u2014 new.\n2. compact: 37F, 48-53, 56F, 60, 62,63F, 70-71, 74-76, 77F, 82,84T,85, 95 \u2014 new.\n3. whitespace around: 38T,39,40, 56T,57,58F, 63T,64,65F, 77T,78,79 \u2014 new.\n4. repeated ws after colon: 65T,66 \u2014 new.\n5. multiple pairs: 86F,88,89,90,91F + second iteration \u2014 new.\n6. invalid prop name: 44T,45-47 \u2014 new.\n7. missing colon: 58T,59 \u2014 new.\n8. missing value: 67,68,72,73 \u2014 new.\n9. unterminated: 80,81,86T,87 \u2014 new.\n10. invalid key after comma: 91T,92-94 \u2014 new.\n11. duplicate keys memo: memo-hit path of 52-53 \u2014 new (behavioral).\n12-13. integration \u2014 wiring.\n\nEvery test adds something. \u2713\n\nNow write the final code. Also add module docstring? Keep minimal. The user said respond only with code in backticks.\n\nLet me write it:\n\n```python\nimport typing\n\nimport pytest\nfrom json.decoder import JSONDecodeError, scanstring\n\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import _TokenizingJSONObject, tokenize_json\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import ScalarToken\n\n\ndef _make_scan_once(content: str) -> typing.Callable[[str, int], typing.Tuple[ScalarToken, int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Minimal stand-in for the module's real scanner: it supports JSON strings\n    and non-negative integers and raises ``StopIteration(idx)`` for anything\n    else, exactly like ``json.scanner`` does (this is what\n    ``_TokenizingJSONObject`` relies on for its error handling).\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def scan_once(s: str, idx: int) -> typing.Tuple[ScalarToken, int]:\n        try:\n            char = s[idx]\n        except IndexError:\n            raise StopIteration(idx) from None\n        if char == '\"':\n            value, end = scanstring(s, idx + 1, True)\n            return ScalarToken(value, idx, end - 1, content), end\n        if char.isdigit():\n            start = idx\n            end = idx + 1\n            while end < len(s) and s[end].isdigit():\n                end += 1\n            return ScalarToken(int(s[start:end]), start, end - 1, content), end\n        raise StopIteration(idx)\n\n    return scan_once\n\n\ndef _parse_object(s: str, start: int = 1) -> typing.Tuple[dict, int]:\n    # A fresh memo/content per call: the function under test keeps no global\n    # state, so no monkeypatching/cleanup is required.\n    return _TokenizingJSONObject((s, start), True, _make_scan_once(s), {}, s)\n\n\ndef _as_pairs(result: dict) -> list:\n    return [(key.value, value.value) for key, value in result.items()]\n\n\ndef test_empty_object_returns_empty_dict_and_end_after_closing_brace():\n    s = \"{}\"\n    result, end = _parse_object(s)\n    assert result == {}\n    assert end == 2\n\n\ndef test_compact_object_fast_paths():\n    s = '{\"a\":1}'\n    memo = {}\n    result, end = _TokenizingJSONObject((s, 1), True, _make_scan_once(s), memo, s)\n    assert _as_pairs(result) == [(\"a\", 1)]\n    assert end == len(s)\n    key = next(iter(result))\n    assert key.value == \"a\"\n    assert (key._start_index, key._end_index) == (1, 3)\n    value = result[key]\n    assert value.value == 1\n    assert (value._start_index, value._end_index) == (5, 5)\n    # The key has been stored in the shared memo dict.\n    assert memo == {\"a\": \"a\"}\n\n\ndef test_whitespace_around_key_colon_and_value():\n    s = '{ \"a\" : 1 }'\n    result, end = _parse_object(s)\n    assert _as_pairs(result) == [(\"a\", 1)]\n    assert end == len(s)\n\n\ndef test_repeated_whitespace_after_colon():\n    s = '{\"a\": \\t\\n1}'\n    result, end = _parse_object(s)\n    assert _as_pairs(result) == [(\"a\", 1)]\n    assert end == len(s)\n\n\ndef test_multiple_pairs_separated_by_comma():\n    s = '{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}'\n    result, end = _parse_object(s)\n    assert _as_pairs(result) == [(\"a\", 1), (\"b\", 2)]\n    assert end == len(s)\n\n\ndef test_duplicate_keys_are_memoized_and_last_pair_wins():\n    s = '{\"a\": 1, \"a\": 2}'\n    result, end = _parse_object(s)\n    assert _as_pairs(result) == [(\"a\", 2)]\n    assert end == len(s)\n\n\ndef test_invalid_property_name_raises():\n    s = \"{1: 2}\"\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _parse_object(s)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 1\n\n\ndef test_missing_colon_raises():\n    s = '{\"a\" 1}'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _parse_object(s)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting ':' delimiter\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 5\n\n\ndef test_missing_value_raises_expecting_value():\n    # Triggers both the IndexError guard after ':' and the\n    # StopIteration -> JSONDecodeError translation.\n    s = '{\"a\":'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _parse_object(s)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting value\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 5\n\n\ndef test_unterminated_object_raises_expecting_comma():\n    # s[end] after the value raises IndexError -> nextchar = \"\" ->\n    # \"Expecting ',' delimiter\".\n    s = '{\"a\": 1'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _parse_object(s)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting ',' delimiter\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 7\n\n\ndef test_invalid_property_name_after_comma_raises():\n    s = '{\"a\": 1, 2}'\n    with pytest.raises(JSONDecodeError) as excinfo:\n        _parse_object(s)\n    assert excinfo.value.msg == \"Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes\"\n    assert excinfo.value.pos == 8\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_json_integration_object():\n    token = tokenize_json('{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert token.value == {\"a\": 1}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_json_integration_parse_error():\n 